Aerie-Purpose: Give dedicated workers the noise tokens of the page that created them, so OffscreenCanvas and navigator.hardwareConcurrency in a worker are noised per site like in a window, and the per-site and global switches reach them.
Aerie-Upstream-Area: third_party/blink/renderer/core/workers (GlobalScopeCreationParams, WorkerGlobalScope, DedicatedWorker)
Aerie-Kind: hook

Amends helium-chromium/patches/helium/core/noise/core.patch (issue #113, part of #87). Helium sets the
noise tokens on a window only (DocumentLoader::CommitNavigation), so a worker returned clean OffscreenCanvas
pixels and the real core count: a fingerprinter only had to move its code into a worker.

GlobalScopeCreationParams gets a plain field, helium_noise_tokens, that is not a constructor argument, so no
creation site changes. WorkerGlobalScope copies it into the ExecutionContext, which is where the canvas
helper, NavigatorBase::hardwareConcurrency and the GPU masking helper already read tokens, so they need no
change. DedicatedWorker::CreateGlobalScope fills it from its creator's ExecutionContext: a window holds its
page's tokens (the top-level site's, derived in the browser), and a worker holds the ones it was given, so a
worker started by a worker gets the same tokens. The tokens are the per-site derived ones; the renderer never
receives the session token. An empty map (protection off for the browser or the site) stays empty in the
worker, so the worker sees the real values. The tokens are a per-worker copy: a later change of the switch
applies to workers started after it, as it does to the next navigation of a window.

Shared and service workers get their tokens from the browser: hardening/0014.
